# ESP32 Hot Flash Simulation

## Project Description

This project uses an ESP32 to simulate the buildup and cooldown of a hot flash using **9 red LEDs and 1 green safety LED**.

The ESP32 communicates with a laptop through **USB serial communication**. Commands sent through the serial monitor control the animation and safety system.

## Hardware Used

- ESP32 development board
- 9 red LEDs
- 1 green LED (safety channel)
- 10 current-limiting, 330 ohm resistors
- Breadboard and jumper wires
- USB-C data cable

### GPIO Configuration

| LEDs | GPIO Pins |
|---|---|
| 9 Red LEDs | 16, 17, 18, 19, 21, 23, 25, 26, 27 |
| Green Safety LED | 22 |

### Circuit Configuration
GPIO16 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 1)
GPIO17 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 2)
GPIO18 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 3)
GPIO19 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 4)
GPIO21 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 5)
GPIO23 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 6)
GPIO25 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 7)
GPIO26 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 8)
GPIO27 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Red LED 9)
GPIO22 ── 330Ω ──>|── GND   (Green Safety LED)

## Technologies and Concepts

**PlatformIO + Arduino Framework**

Used to develop, compile, and upload the firmware to the ESP32.

**PWM (Pulse Width Modulation)**

The ESP32's LEDC peripheral controls the brightness of all 9 red LEDs independently, creating smooth fading effects instead of simple ON/OFF transitions.

**Non-blocking Timing (`millis()`)**

The animation uses `millis()` instead of `delay()`. This allows the ESP32 to continue processing serial commands while the LED sequence is running, keeping the STOP command responsive.

**USB Serial Communication**

The ESP32 receives text commands from a laptop through the USB serial connection at 115200 baud.

**Automatic Safety Timeout**

A watchdog-style software timer automatically disables the outputs if no valid command refreshes the safety timer for 5 seconds.

## Available Commands

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `START` | Starts/restarts the hot-flash LED animation and activates the green safety LED |
| `STOP` | Interrupts the animation and switches off all LEDs |
| `PING` | Resets the 5-second safety timer |
| `STATUS` | Displays the current animation and safety states |
| `HELP` | Lists available commands |

While the safety channel is active, `START`, `PING`, `STATUS`, and `HELP` refresh the timeout. Invalid commands do not.

## Hot Flash Animation

The animation consists of three stages:

1. **Build-up:** The red LEDs gradually illuminate one after another.
2. **Peak:** All 9 LEDs reach maximum brightness.
3. **Cooldown:** The LEDs gradually fade out in reverse order.

The complete animation takes approximately **4.38 seconds**.

The green LED represents a separate safety-controlled channel. It remains active until a STOP command or the 5-second inactivity timeout.

## Challenges and Implementation Notes

- Hardware part was pretty straightforward so main part of the task was the software part.
- **Responsive command handling:** The main design challenge was ensuring the animation would not block incoming STOP commands. Using `millis()` and non-blocking PWM updates allows animation and serial communication to operate together.
- **Safety behavior:** The safety timer is managed independently of the animation, allowing the green LED to remain active after the animation finishes and switch off automatically when communication stops.

## Building and Running

1. Open the project in VS Code with PlatformIO.
2. Select `Espressif ESP32 Dev Module` (`esp32dev`) and the Arduino framework.
3. Connect the ESP32 using a USB data cable.
4. Build and upload the firmware.
5. Open the serial monitor at **115200 baud**.
6. Send `START`, `STOP`, or other supported commands.

